#include <iostream>

#define HALF_BAD(x) x / 2
#define HALF(x) ((x) / 2)
#define MIN(a, b) ((a) < (b) ? (a) : (b))

int minOf(int a, int b) {
    return a < b ? a : b;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << "HALF_BAD(10 + 4): " << HALF_BAD(10 + 4) << '\n';
    std::cout << "HALF(10 + 4): " << HALF(10 + 4) << '\n';

    int bid = 3;
    const int low = MIN(bid--, 9);
    std::cout << "macro: low " << low << ", bid " << bid << '\n';

    int bid2 = 3;
    const int low2 = minOf(bid2--, 9);
    std::cout << "function: low " << low2 << ", bid " << bid2 << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
`#if` keeps or drops lines before the compiler runs, so code meant for another platform never has to compile at all:

```cpp harness
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
#if defined(__APPLE__)
    std::cout << "macOS\n";
#elif defined(__linux__)
    std::cout << "Linux\n";  // the sandbox
#else
    std::cout << "something else\n";
#endif
```

`#ifdef NAME` is short for `#if defined(NAME)`, and `#ifndef NAME` for `#if !defined(NAME)`. `#if` also compares whole numbers and combines tests with `&&`, `||`, and `!`. Every compiler predefines macros that describe the build:

| Macro | Tells you |
| --- | --- |
| `__cplusplus` | the standard: `201703L` for C++17 |
| `__linux__`, `__APPLE__`, `_WIN32` | the OS is Linux, macOS, or Windows |
| `__x86_64__`, `__aarch64__` | the CPU is x86-64 or ARM64 (MSVC: `_M_X64`, `_M_ARM64`) |
| `__clang__`, `__GNUC__`, `_MSC_VER` | the compiler is clang, g++, or MSVC |
| `__FILE__`, `__LINE__` | the current file and line |

Two traps hide in that table. clang defines `__GNUC__` too, as 4, for old code that checks it, so test `__clang__` first. And `__cplusplus` is the standard you compile with, not the newest your compiler knows. Plain `clang++` on the Mac builds C++14, as *From Source to Executable: Preprocess, Compile, Link* showed, so it reports `201402`. g++ 10, whose C++20 was unfinished, reports `201709` even with `-std=c++20`. `#error` stops the build with your message, here because the C++17 sandbox is asked for C++20:

```cpp error
#include <iostream>

#if __cplusplus < 202002L
#error "this code needs C++20"
#endif

int main() {
    std::cout << "built as C++20\n";
    return 0;
}
```

`clang++ -std=c++17 -dM -E -x c++ /dev/null` lists everything your compiler predefines, 545 macros on the Mac. `__has_include(<header>)` tests whether a header exists, and only that: with `-std=c++17` on the Mac, `__has_include(<format>)` is true, though `std::format` needs C++20. *Designing for Low Latency*, in the advanced track, uses `#if defined(__x86_64__)` to pick the right CPU instruction on each architecture.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

#if __cplusplus < 201703L
#error "this code needs C++17: build with -std=c++17"
#endif

int main() {
#if defined(__APPLE__)
    std::cout << "OS: macOS\n";
#elif defined(__linux__)
    std::cout << "OS: Linux\n";
#elif defined(_WIN32)
    std::cout << "OS: Windows\n";
#else
    std::cout << "OS: unknown\n";
#endif

#if defined(__x86_64__) || defined(_M_X64)
    std::cout << "CPU: x86-64\n";
#elif defined(__aarch64__) || defined(_M_ARM64)
    std::cout << "CPU: ARM64\n";
#else
    std::cout << "CPU: unknown\n";
#endif

#if defined(__clang__)
    std::cout << "Compiler: clang " << __clang_major__ << '\n';
#elif defined(__GNUC__)
    std::cout << "Compiler: g++ " << __GNUC__ << '.' << __GNUC_MINOR__ << '\n';
#elif defined(_MSC_VER)
    std::cout << "Compiler: MSVC " << _MSC_VER << '\n';
#endif
    std::cout << "C++: " << __cplusplus << '\n';
    return 0;
}

